            {
              "id": "e15d1418-850f-306d-a45a-09594367bcac",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Identified\" vulnerability refers to the use of C functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, memory leaks, and other security issues. In this case, the `std::strftime` function is used, which can lead to buffer overflow if the size of the output exceeds the size of the buffer.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions that include checks for buffer sizes and other potential issues. In C++, this often means using functions and classes provided by the Standard Template Library (STL) that are designed with safety in mind.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `std::strftime` function with a safer alternative, such as `std::put_time`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n#include <iomanip>\n#include <ctime>\n\nstd::time_t tt = std::time(nullptr);\nstd::tm* tm = std::localtime(&tt);\nstd::cout << std::put_time(tm, \"%m/%d/%Y %H:%M:%S\") << std::endl;\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<ctime>`: for `std::time`, `std::localtime`, and `std::tm`\n- `<iomanip>`: for `std::put_time`\n- `<iostream>`: for `std::cout` and `std::endl`\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "1be7e934-72b1-3a74-85a9-791331d746d6",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In the provided code snippet, the `wcslen` function is used, which can lead to buffer overflow if the input string is not null-terminated.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using C library functions that do not perform bounds checking. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ Standard Library. In this case, instead of `wcslen`, use `std::wstring::length` or `std::wstring::size`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `wcslen` function with `std::wstring::length` or `std::wstring::size`. Here is the fixed code:\n\n```cpp\nstd::wstring wname = string(name);\niter = dict->find(wname);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<string>`: For `std::string` and `std::wstring`.\n- `<map>`: If `dict` is a `std::map`.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "7432addc-a970-3c8a-a13c-55592209a140",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen` function in C and C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string until it reaches a null character (`'\\0'`). If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen` will continue reading memory until it encounters a null character, which can lead to buffer overflows, memory corruption, and other unexpected behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that your strings are null-terminated. This can be done by initializing your strings with a null character or by manually adding a null character at the end of your string. Additionally, consider using safer alternatives to `strlen`, such as `strnlen`, which takes a maximum length parameter and will not read past this length, even if it does not encounter a null character.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n// Ensure that closingBraceVar is null-terminated\nclosingBraceVar[closingBraceVarSize - 1] = '\\0';\nclosingBraceVarSize = strlen(closingBraceVar);\n```\n\nOr use `strnlen`:\n\n```cpp\n// Use strnlen instead of strlen\nclosingBraceVarSize = strnlen(closingBraceVar, MAX_SIZE);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strlen` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ea99cb13-b3e5-35b6-92d4-2b717eb8654a",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen()` function in C and C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string until it reaches a null character (`'\\0'`). If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen()` will continue reading memory until it encounters a null character, which can lead to buffer overflows, memory corruption, and other undefined behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that your strings are null-terminated. Avoid using `strlen()` on strings that may not be null-terminated. Instead, consider using functions that take the size of the buffer as an argument, such as `strnlen()`, which will not read past the specified length, even if it does not encounter a null character.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t openBraceVarSize = strnlen(openBraceVar, MAX_SIZE);\n```\n\nIn this code, `MAX_SIZE` should be the maximum size of `openBraceVar`. This ensures that `strnlen()` will not read past the end of the buffer.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<cstring>` library in C++.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-125: Out-of-bounds Read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/125.html)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are valid and active at the time of writing."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "7ff4fa54-260f-3341-9cb2-72853028f776",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Identified\" is a vulnerability that occurs when a program uses a function that is considered unsafe due to its potential for causing buffer overflow errors. This vulnerability can lead to serious security issues such as code execution, denial of service, or information disclosure.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the function `wcslen(name)` is used, which is a potential source of buffer overflow vulnerabilities. This function does not perform any bounds checking and can therefore lead to buffer overflows if the input is not properly validated.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives that perform bounds checking, such as `strnlen` or `strncpy`. These functions limit the number of characters to be copied, thus preventing buffer overflow.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `wcslen(name)` function with a safer alternative. Here is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid ExistsCurrent(std::wstring name) {\n    size_t length = std::min(name.length(), wcsnlen(name.c_str(), name.length()));\n    std::wstring safe_name(name.c_str(), name.c_str() + length);\n    // ...\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- `<string>`: for `std::wstring` and `std::string` types.\n- `<algorithm>`: for `std::min` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "796646b8-21ee-305e-a114-8263e83efdbd",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Identified\" is a vulnerability that arises when a C++ program uses certain C library functions that are considered unsafe. These functions are unsafe because they do not perform bounds checking or other necessary validation, which can lead to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflows.\n\nIn this case, the function `atoi(s)` is used, which converts a string to an integer. This function does not check whether the conversion was successful or not, and if the string cannot be converted, it returns zero. This can lead to unexpected behavior if not handled properly.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using unsafe C library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives that perform necessary validation. In the case of `atoi(s)`, a safer alternative is `strtol(s, &endptr, 10)`. This function returns a long integer, and if the conversion is unsuccessful, `endptr` will not point to the null terminator.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `atoi(s)` function with `strtol(s, &endptr, 10)`. Here is an example:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib>\n#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    const char* s = \"123\";\n    char* endptr;\n    long int value = strtol(s, &endptr, 10);\n    if (*endptr != '\\0') {\n        std::cerr << \"Conversion error, non-convertible part: \" << endptr;\n    }\n    // use value...\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `strtol(s, &endptr, 10)` is used to convert the string to a long integer. If the conversion is unsuccessful, `endptr` will not point to the null terminator, and an error message will be printed.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdlib>`: This header file contains declarations of general functions, including `strtol()`.\n- `<iostream>`: This header file defines the standard input/output stream objects.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a59be220-fee1-3b4b-a48e-9b972adf495d",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other problems. One such function is `strtok`.\n\nThe `strtok` function is used to split a string into tokens but it is not thread-safe. If it is used in multi-threaded applications, it can cause data races and lead to undefined behavior. It also modifies the input string, which can lead to unexpected behavior if the input string is not supposed to be modified.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `strtok` function. Instead, use safer alternatives such as `strtok_r` or `strtok_s` which are reentrant and safer to use in multi-threaded applications. Also, consider using C++ string handling functions or libraries that provide safer string manipulation functions.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `strtok`, you can use `strtok_r` as shown below:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n\nchar str[] = \"A string\\tof ,,tokens\\nand some  more tokens\";\nchar delims[] = \" \\t,.\\n\";\nchar *token = NULL;\nchar *saveptr = NULL;\n\nfor (token = strtok_r(str, delims, &saveptr); token != NULL; token = strtok_r(NULL, delims, &saveptr)) {\n    // process token\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `cstring` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189",
                  "a59be220-fee1-3b4b-a48e-9b972adf495d"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "57075a9e-3ae1-3859-b515-f0b6b5c46751",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other problems. One such function is `strtok`.\n\nThe `strtok` function is used to split a string into tokens but it is not thread-safe. If it is used in multi-threaded applications, it can cause data races and lead to undefined behavior. It also modifies the input string, which can lead to unexpected behavior if the input string is not supposed to be modified.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `strtok` function. Instead, use safer alternatives such as `strtok_r` or `strtok_s` which are reentrant and safer to use in multi-threaded applications. Also, consider using C++ string handling functions or libraries that provide safer string manipulation functions.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `strtok`, you can use `strtok_r` as shown below:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n\nchar str[] = \"A string\\tof ,,tokens\\nand some  more tokens\";\nchar delims[] = \" \\t,.\\n\";\nchar *token = NULL;\nchar *saveptr = NULL;\n\nfor (token = strtok_r(str, delims, &saveptr); token != NULL; token = strtok_r(NULL, delims, &saveptr)) {\n    // process token\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `cstring` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
