            {
              "id": "b0a9af99-c9ff-38b2-bc0f-687ed25d4e4a",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, the use of non-cryptographic random number generators such as `rand()` can lead to vulnerabilities. These generators are not designed to be unpredictable and can produce predictable values, which can be exploited by an attacker. This is especially critical when the random numbers are used for security-critical operations, such as generating encryption keys, session identifiers, or other security tokens.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use a cryptographic random number generator that is designed to be unpredictable and resistant to attack. In C++, you can use functions from the `<random>` library, such as `std::random_device`, `std::mt19937` (Mersenne Twister 19937 generator), or other cryptographic libraries like OpenSSL's `RAND_bytes`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `rand()` function with a cryptographic random number generator. Here is an example using `std::random_device` and `std::mt19937`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 gen(rd());\nstd::uniform_int_distribution<> distr(0, RAND_MAX);\n\nrandno = distr(gen) * RAND_MAX + distr(gen);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<random>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
