            {
              "id": "65b61415-ff7a-342d-ac2b-d3589496eddc",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ programming language refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other problems. In this case, the vulnerability sink is `m_filename.c_str()`, which is a method that returns a pointer to an array that contains a null-terminated sequence of characters representing the current value of the string object.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nThe general mitigation advice for this vulnerability is to avoid using C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ standard library. In this case, instead of using `c_str()`, consider using `std::string` methods that do not involve C-style strings.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `m_filename.c_str()`, consider using `m_filename` directly if the API you are using supports `std::string`. If you need to pass a C-style string to an API that does not support `std::string`, consider using `std::string::data()` instead, which also returns a pointer to the string's contents as a C-style string.\n\n```cpp\n// Instead of this:\nconst char* filename = m_filename.c_str();\n\n// Do this if possible:\nstd::string filename = m_filename;\n\n// Or this if you need a C-style string:\nconst char* filename = m_filename.data();\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies to execute properly:\n\n- `<string>`\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
