            {
              "id": "99b6027e-48bf-3b72-ae93-b2ab9345b9bd",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C/C++ Standard Library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the `wcscpy` function is used, which can lead to buffer overflow if the source string is larger than the destination buffer.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using insecure C/C++ Standard Library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives that perform bounds checking to prevent buffer overflows. In this case, you can use `wcsncpy` instead of `wcscpy`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `wcscpy` function with `wcsncpy` and ensure that the size of the destination buffer is not exceeded. Here is the fixed code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n#include <string>\n\n// ...\n\nwchar_t buf[BUFSIZE];\nstd::wstring filepath;\n\n// ...\n\nwcsncpy(buf, filepath.c_str(), BUFSIZE - 1);\nbuf[BUFSIZE - 1] = '\\0';  // Ensure null termination\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstring>`: for `wcsncpy` function\n- `<string>`: for `std::wstring`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links are valid and active for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "0ac90d8d-e46e-3075-94c0-dea6e97541a2",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues that can lead to arbitrary code execution or denial of service. In this case, the function `strtok` is considered unsafe because it is not thread-safe and can lead to data races if used in a multi-threaded context.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to `strtok`. In C++, you can use `std::string` and its member functions to perform string manipulations in a safer and more idiomatic way. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `strtok`, you can use `std::getline` in combination with a `std::stringstream` to split a string by a delimiter. Here is an example:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string>\n#include <sstream>\n#include <vector>\n\nstd::string s = \"your string here\";\nstd::vector<std::string> tokens;\nstd::string token;\nstd::istringstream tokenStream(s);\nwhile (std::getline(tokenStream, token, '\\n')) {\n    tokens.push_back(token);\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `tokens` will contain all the tokens in the string `s` that are separated by the newline character.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<string>`\n- `<sstream>`\n- `<vector>`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189",
                  "0ac90d8d-e46e-3075-94c0-dea6e97541a2"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b125e1ab-3f1b-3087-8106-787300f43ac2",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues that can lead to arbitrary code execution or denial of service. In this case, the function `strtok` is considered unsafe because it is not thread-safe and can lead to data races if used in a multi-threaded context.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to `strtok`. In C++, you can use `std::string` and its member functions to perform string manipulations in a safer and more idiomatic way. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `strtok`, you can use `std::getline` in combination with a `std::stringstream` to split a string by a delimiter. Here is an example:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string>\n#include <sstream>\n#include <vector>\n\nstd::string s = \"your string here\";\nstd::vector<std::string> tokens;\nstd::string token;\nstd::istringstream tokenStream(s);\nwhile (std::getline(tokenStream, token, '\\n')) {\n    tokens.push_back(token);\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `tokens` will contain all the tokens in the string `s` that are separated by the newline character.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<string>`\n- `<sstream>`\n- `<vector>`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
