            {
              "id": "f6c5c0c5-e766-3d8e-9b7f-c4ea90fa3cbe",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the `ungetc()` function is used, which can lead to runtime errors if not used properly.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using insecure C library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ Standard Library. Always validate and sanitize input data. Be aware of the potential risks associated with the functions you are using and implement error handling to manage potential issues.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe `ungetc()` function pushes the character back to the stream and can return EOF if unsuccessful. Therefore, it's important to check the return value of `ungetc()` and handle the error appropriately. Here is a simple fix:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdio>\n#include <cstring>\n#include <stdexcept>\n\n// ...\n\nint result = ungetc(c, stream);\nif (result == EOF) {\n    throw std::runtime_error(\"error in ungetc(): \" + std::string(strerror(errno)));\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n- `<cstdio>`: Provides functions for file input and output.\n- `<cstring>`: Provides functions to manipulate C strings and arrays.\n- `<stdexcept>`: Provides a set of standard exceptions that can be thrown from a program.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "95e367dd-983c-3879-a691-57c4582794c2",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues that can lead to arbitrary code execution or denial of service. In this case, the `ftell()` function is used, which is generally safe but can cause issues if the file position cannot be represented correctly.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions, if available. For instance, instead of `ftell()`, consider using `fseek()` or `fgetpos()`. Always check the return values of these functions to ensure they have completed successfully. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the provided code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdio>\n#include <cstring>\n#include <stdexcept>\n\nFILE* file = fopen(\"file.txt\", \"r\");\nif (file == NULL) {\n    throw std::runtime_error(\"Failed to open file\");\n}\n\nif (fseek(file, 0, SEEK_END) != 0) {\n    fclose(file);\n    throw std::runtime_error(\"Failed to seek to end of file\");\n}\n\nlong position = ftell(file);\nif (position == -1L) {\n    fclose(file);\n    throw std::runtime_error(std::string(\"Error in ftell(): \") + strerror(errno));\n}\n\nfclose(file);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- cstdio\n- cstring\n- stdlib\n- stdexcept\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
