            {
              "id": "28d7e3ba-59e0-389d-9499-07703dc52418",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. These functions are often prohibited in secure coding standards due to their potential misuse or abuse.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the function `strerror(errno)` is used, which is not thread-safe and can lead to race conditions if used in a multi-threaded environment. This function returns a pointer to a string that describes the error code passed in the argument errno. If multiple threads call this function, they might overwrite each other's data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using non-thread-safe functions in a multi-threaded environment. Instead, use thread-safe alternatives. In this case, use `strerror_r(errno, buf, sizeof buf)` instead of `strerror(errno)`. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strerror(errno)` function with `strerror_r(errno, buf, sizeof buf)`. Here is the corrected code:\n\n```cpp\nchar buf[1024];\nRuntimeError(\"error resetting file position: %s\", strerror_r(errno, buf, sizeof buf));\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- `<errno.h>`: for the `errno` variable.\n- `<string.h>`: for the `strerror` or `strerror_r` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
