            {
              "id": "71b14967-1342-3d9e-a1e6-bdd6df7e0860",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflows. In this case, the `vfprintf` function is used, which can lead to format string vulnerabilities if the format string is not controlled by the programmer or if user-supplied data is used as a format string.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using the `vfprintf` function with user-supplied data. Instead, use safer alternatives such as `vsnprintf` or `vasprintf` which do not have these vulnerabilities. Always validate and sanitize user input to prevent injection attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `vfprintf` function with a safer alternative. For example:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdarg.h>\n\nvoid safe_printf(FILE* f, const char* fmt, ...) {\n    va_list arg_ptr;\n    va_start(arg_ptr, fmt);\n    char* buffer;\n    vasprintf(&buffer, fmt, arg_ptr);\n    va_end(arg_ptr);\n    fputs(buffer, f);\n    free(buffer);\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- stdio.h\n- stdarg.h\n- stdlib.h\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
