            {
              "id": "3b04371c-cb2c-32c8-9078-74bf7c3bd81d",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Identified\" is a vulnerability that occurs when a C++ program uses a function that is considered unsafe due to its potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other security issues. In this case, the `fwscanf` function is used, which can lead to these types of vulnerabilities if not used carefully. \n\nThe `fwscanf` function reads formatted input from a file. If the format string is not properly validated or is controlled by an attacker, it can lead to a format string vulnerability. This can allow an attacker to read arbitrary memory, cause a crash, or execute arbitrary code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using `fwscanf` and other unsafe C functions. Instead, use safer alternatives that perform bounds checking or that do not have the same vulnerabilities. \n\nIn addition, always validate and sanitize input, including format strings. Never trust input from an untrusted source without validating it first. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `fwscanf` function with a safer alternative. For example, you could use `fscanf` with a specified field width to prevent buffer overflows:\n\n```cpp\n#define MAX_WIDTH 100\nwchar_t v[MAX_WIDTH];\nrc = fwscanf(f, L\"%100ls\", v);\n```\n\nThis code limits the input to 100 wide characters, preventing a buffer overflow.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cwchar>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
