            {
              "id": "b8fb244d-bf7d-322f-b99a-380cfec26428",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The destination and source may refer to overlapping memory, as indicated by the data flow analysis. Additionally, there are no explicit checks to ensure that neither the source nor the destination pointers are null before the operation, which further increases the risk of undefined behavior or a crash. The absence of bounds checks or guards to verify the validity and size of the buffers involved means that this code is susceptible to memory corruption or program instability if the overlap or null pointer conditions occur at runtime.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory, ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap. If overlap is possible, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`, as `memmove` is safe for overlapping regions. Additionally, add explicit checks to ensure that both the source and destination pointers are not null before performing the copy operation. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif (rowIndices.data() != nullptr && rawRowIndices != nullptr) {\n    // If overlap is possible, use memmove instead of memcpy\n    memmove(rowIndices.data(), rawRowIndices, numNonZeroValues * sizeof(SparseIndexType));\n}\n```\n\nIf you are certain that overlap cannot occur, document this assumption and add assertions to enforce it at runtime:\n\n```cpp\nassert(rowIndices.data() != nullptr);\nassert(rawRowIndices != nullptr);\n// Optionally, assert that regions do not overlap\nassert(rowIndices.data() + numNonZeroValues <= rawRowIndices || rawRowIndices + numNonZeroValues <= rowIndices.data());\nmemcpy(rowIndices.data(), rawRowIndices, numNonZeroValues * sizeof(SparseIndexType));\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "422739ad-9635-37bd-bd13-bd7b81557fff",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The destination and source may refer to overlapping memory, as indicated by the dataflow analysis. Additionally, there are no checks ensuring that the source and destination pointers are non-null before the operation, which increases the risk of null pointer dereference. The absence of explicit bounds checks or guards further increases the likelihood of a real issue. The verdict is supported by the classification and the high-confidence 'GENUINE' label from the analysis.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nBefore performing the memory copy, ensure that the source and destination pointers are not null and that the memory regions do not overlap. If overlap is possible, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`, as `memmove` is safe for overlapping regions. Also, validate that the number of elements to copy does not exceed the capacity of the destination buffer.\n\n```cpp\nif (nonZeroValues.data() != nullptr && rawNonZeroValues != nullptr) {\n    // If overlap is possible, use memmove instead of memcpy\n    memmove(nonZeroValues.data(), rawNonZeroValues, numNonZeroValues * sizeof(ElementType));\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "5634067f-df82-320c-bedf-b55031851772",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The destination and source may refer to overlapping memory, as indicated by the dataflow analysis. Additionally, there are no checks ensuring that the source and destination pointers are non-null, which increases the risk of null pointer dereference. The absence of explicit bounds or capacity checks for the destination buffer further increases the risk. The verdict is supported by the classification and the high-confidence 'GENUINE' label from the analysis.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory, ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap. If overlap is possible, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`, as `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping regions safely. Additionally, add explicit checks to ensure that neither the source nor the destination pointers are null before performing the copy operation. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif (colStarts.data() != nullptr && rawColStarts != nullptr) {\n    memmove(colStarts.data(), rawColStarts, numOfColsInMatrix * sizeof(SparseIndexType));\n}\n```\n\nThis change ensures safe copying even if the memory regions overlap and prevents null pointer dereference. Always validate buffer sizes to avoid buffer overflows.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
